\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Closed orbit and limit sets}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Let #{\varphi^{t}} be a \vocabk{flow}{fnip-0004} in the plane. A
\newvocab{closed orbit} is the image of a solution that is not an
\vocabk{equilibrium}{fnip-0014} and that returns to its starting
state after a positive time.}

\p{The \newvocab{omega-limit set} of a state #{x} is the set of states
#{y} for which some sequence of times tending to #{+\infty} carries
#{\varphi^{t}(x)} to #{y}. The \newvocab{alpha-limit set} of #{x} is
the set of states #{y} for which some sequence of times tending to
#{-\infty} carries #{\varphi^{t}(x)} to #{y}.}

\p{\citet{§1.6, p. 34}{guckenheimer1983nonlinear} defines the
#{\omega}-limit set and the #{\alpha}-limit set by sequences of times
tending to #{+\infty} and to #{-\infty}.
\citet{Definition 1.4, p. 9}{kuznetsov2004elements} calls a
nonequilibrium orbit that returns after a positive time a cycle, and
in continuous time that orbit is a closed curve.
\citet{§1.5, p. 22}{guckenheimer1983nonlinear} studies the stability
of such orbits.
\citet{§4.1.3, p. 96}{izhikevich2007dynamical} calls the trajectory a
periodic trajectory.}
